# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# LOCAL DEVELOPMENT TEMPLATE — copy to .env.local at the repo root.
#
# The repo is a monorepo with TWO deployments — the storefront (apps/store) and
# the admin panel (apps/admin). Locally both read this one file (each app dir
# has a .env.local symlink back to it). In production they are separate
# processes with separate environments, so every variable has to be set on the
# deployment(s) marked below:
#
#   [both]  set on the storefront AND the admin deployment
#   [store] storefront only
#   [admin] admin only
#
# A variable set on only one of them is invisible to the other. That is what
# left the admin panel half-configured after it was split out of the storefront.
#
# WHERE PRODUCTION VALUES GO
#   Hostinger VPS  apps/store/.env.production and apps/admin/.env.production,
#                  templates in deploy/env/ — see deploy/README.md
#   Vercel         Project → Settings → Environment Variables, per project
#
# CHECK WHAT IS MISSING
#   npm run check-env          strict, both apps, production files
#   npm run check-env:dev      the same against your .env.local
#
# The authoritative list of variables (which app, required vs optional, what
# breaks without it) is packages/core/src/lib/env.ts. Add new variables there
# too, or they will not be checked.
#
# EDITABLE FROM THE ADMIN PANEL
#   STORE_GSTIN, STORE_STATE, STORE_ADDRESS, SUPPORT_EMAIL, ORDER_NOTIFY_EMAIL,
#   BREVO_LIST_ID and GST_RATE can be changed in Settings -> Business details without a
#   redeploy. A value saved there is stored in the `settings` table and WINS
#   over the variable below; clearing the field hands control back here.
#   Everything else is env-only, on purpose: secrets stay out of the database,
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_* is baked in at build time, and ADMIN_URL / STORE_URL are
#   infrastructure wiring you do not want to break from inside the panel.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# [both] Razorpay — get from https://dashboard.razorpay.com → Settings → API Keys
# Use test keys (rzp_test_...) while developing.
RAZORPAY_KEY_ID=rzp_test_xxxxxxxxxxxx
RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET=your_key_secret

# [store] Set in Razorpay Dashboard → Settings → Webhooks when you add the URL.
# Webhook URL: https://<your-domain>/api/webhooks/razorpay
# Events to enable: payment.captured, payment.failed, refund.processed, refund.failed
RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=any_long_random_string

# [store] Secret for the payment-reconciliation cron (/api/cron/reconcile).
# The caller sends "Authorization: Bearer <CRON_SECRET>"; the route fails closed
# and rejects everything if this is unset. Use a long random string.
# On Vercel the caller is Vercel Cron (apps/store/vercel.json); on the VPS it is
# deploy/cron/reconcile.sh, which reads this value out of the env file itself.
CRON_SECRET=another_long_random_string

# [admin] One-time setup secret: the first visit to the admin panel's /login
# uses this to create the owner account. After that it's inert (sign-in is
# email + password).
ADMIN_KEY=change_me_to_a_long_random_string

# [both] Postgres connection string (e.g. Neon via Vercel Marketplace).
# REQUIRED — orders, products, customers and admin accounts all live here, and
# both apps talk to the same database. Run `npm run db:migrate` once.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:pass@host/db

# ── Cross-app wiring (new: the admin panel is a separate deployment) ────────
# [both] The admin panel's own URL. Used for the "open in admin panel" button
# in the new-order email, the low-stock email, the teammate-welcome email and
# the password-reset link. Keep this domain private — it is the whole point of
# splitting the panel out. Unset falls back to NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL.
# ADMIN_URL=https://admin.yourdomain.com
#
# [admin] The storefront's URL, used to push cache invalidation when a price or
# stock level changes. Unset falls back to NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL.
# STORE_URL=https://yourdomain.com
#
# [both] Shared secret for that invalidation call: the admin panel sends it as
# a bearer token to the storefront's POST /api/revalidate, which fails closed
# without it. Must be IDENTICAL on both projects. Use a long random string.
# Unset = catalogue edits still save, they just won't refresh the storefront
# until its next deploy.
# REVALIDATE_SECRET=a_third_long_random_string

# ── Transactional email (Brevo — https://www.brevo.com) ────────────────────
# [both] Both apps send email: the storefront on payment (order confirmation,
# new-order alert), the admin panel on shipping/refund/reset/teammate actions.
# Optional: without BREVO_API_KEY no emails are sent (everything else works).
# Brevo's free tier (300 emails/day) covers a launching D2C store. Get the key
# from Brevo → Settings → SMTP & API → API Keys (starts with xkeysib-).
#
# Deliverability depends far more on domain auth than the provider: verify your
# sending domain in Brevo (Senders, Domains & Dedicated IPs → Domains) and add
# the SPF, DKIM and DMARC records it gives you. EMAIL_FROM must use that domain.
# BREVO_API_KEY=xkeysib-xxxxxxxx
# EMAIL_FROM="EVHERFIT <orders@yourdomain.com>"   # must be on a verified domain
# SUPPORT_EMAIL=support@yourdomain.com            # Reply-To on customer emails
# ORDER_NOTIFY_EMAIL=owner@yourdomain.com         # new-order + low-stock alerts
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://yourdomain.com     # storefront URL; links in emails
#
# [store] Optional: numeric id of a Brevo contact list (Brevo → Contacts → Lists).
# When set, every paying customer is upserted into that list (name + phone) so
# the store can send campaigns later. Unset = no contacts are synced.
# Storefront only: the sync hangs off sendOrderNotifications, which runs on the
# payment paths (verify, webhook, reconcile). Manual sales booked in the admin
# panel do not sync a contact, so setting this on the admin app does nothing.
# BREVO_LIST_ID=2

# [admin] Optional: printed on shipping labels as the return address.
# STORE_ADDRESS=12 MG Road, Bengaluru 560001

# ── Reporting & accounting ─────────────────────────────── [admin] ─────────
# Timezone used to bucket revenue into days/months (admin dashboard & revenue
# page). Defaults to Asia/Kolkata (IST) — the store runs in India but Postgres
# runs in UTC, so this keeps late-night sales in the right IST day/month.
# REPORT_TIMEZONE=Asia/Kolkata
#
# Fallback GST rate for NEW sales. Prices are GST-inclusive. Default 0.18 (18%).
# Editable in Settings -> Business details, which overrides this.
#
# Every order snapshots the rate it was charged onto orders.gst_rate when it is
# paid (migration 0009), and invoices, revenue reports and the CSV export all
# read that per-order rate. Changing this therefore affects future sales only —
# it can never restate an invoice already issued to a customer.
# GST_RATE=0.18
#
# For a compliant tax invoice, set the seller's GST details. STORE_STATE lets
# the invoice split CGST+SGST (intra-state) vs IGST (inter-state) by comparing
# to the customer's state; STORE_GSTIN and HSN_CODE are printed on the invoice.
# STORE_STATE=Karnataka
# STORE_GSTIN=29ABCDE1234F1Z5
# HSN_CODE=9506               # sports/fitness equipment

# ── Analytics (Google Tag Manager) ──────────────────────── [store] ────────
# Optional: set the GTM container id to activate the tag. GA4, Search Console
# and Microsoft Clarity are all added as tags INSIDE this container — no code
# changes needed. Unset = no tracking scripts load (dev/preview stay clean).
# GA4 e-commerce events (view_item, begin_checkout, add_payment_info,
# purchase) are already pushed to the dataLayer by the storefront.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GTM_ID=GTM-XXXXXXX
